## 3. Download and Install OKD Client Tools

After selecting the OKD release, download the installation tools that correspond to the selected OKD release.

For this lab:

```text
OKD Release : 4.22.0-okd-scos.7
Architecture: x86_64
Host OS     : Linux
```

The two primary tools required are:

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `openshift-install` | Creates the OKD installation configuration and Ignition files and manages the installation |
| `oc` | Command-line client used to interact with and administer the OKD cluster |

OKD documentation recommends using the appropriate `oc` version for the OKD release rather than an older client version. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

### 3.1 Create a Tools Directory

Create a dedicated directory for the OKD installation tools:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/okd-tools
cd ~/okd-tools
```

Verify:

```bash
pwd
```

Expected:

```text
/home/<user>/okd-tools
```

---

### 3.2 Set the OKD Release Version

Set the exact release selected in Section 2:

```bash
export OKD_RELEASE=4.22.0-okd-scos.7
```

Verify:

```bash
echo "$OKD_RELEASE"
```

Expected:

```text
4.22.0-okd-scos.7
```

Also verify the host architecture:

```bash
uname -m
```

Expected:

```text
x86_64
```

---

### 3.3 Download the OKD Client (`oc`)

The OKD release provides a Linux x86_64 client archive.

Download the client archive for the selected release:

```bash
curl -LO https://github.com/okd-project/okd/releases/download/${OKD_RELEASE}/openshift-client-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

Verify that the archive was downloaded:

```bash
ls -lh openshift-client-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

You should see the downloaded archive.

---

### 3.4 Extract the `oc` Client

Extract the archive:

```bash
tar -xzf openshift-client-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

Verify the extracted files:

```bash
ls -lh
```

You should see:

```text
oc
kubectl
```

Make the binaries executable:

```bash
chmod +x oc kubectl
```

Verify:

```bash
ls -l oc kubectl
```

The permissions should include the executable bit, for example:

```text
-rwxr-xr-x
```

---

### 3.5 Install `oc` into the PATH

Check the current PATH:

```bash
echo "$PATH"
```

A common location for manually installed user binaries is:

```text
/usr/local/bin
```

If you have administrative privileges, copy the binaries:

```bash
sudo cp oc kubectl /usr/local/bin/
```

Verify:

```bash
which oc
which kubectl
```

Expected:

```text
/usr/local/bin/oc
/usr/local/bin/kubectl
```

---

### 3.6 Verify the `oc` Version

Run:

```bash
oc version --client
```

Also run:

```bash
oc version --client=true
```

The output should show the client version corresponding to the selected OKD release.

You can also verify:

```bash
oc version --client -o yaml
```

This provides more detailed version information.

> Do not use an old `oc` binary left on the system if it does not correspond to the OKD release being installed.

---

### 3.7 Download the OKD Installation Program

Download the matching OKD installation program:

```bash
curl -LO https://github.com/okd-project/okd/releases/download/${OKD_RELEASE}/openshift-install-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

Verify the archive:

```bash
ls -lh openshift-install-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

---

### 3.8 Extract `openshift-install`

Extract the installer:

```bash
tar -xzf openshift-install-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

Verify:

```bash
ls -lh openshift-install
```

Make it executable:

```bash
chmod +x openshift-install
```

Verify the permission:

```bash
ls -l openshift-install
```

Expected permissions should include:

```text
-rwxr-xr-x
```

---

### 3.9 Install `openshift-install` into the PATH

Copy the installer to `/usr/local/bin`:

```bash
sudo cp openshift-install /usr/local/bin/
```

Verify its location:

```bash
which openshift-install
```

Expected:

```text
/usr/local/bin/openshift-install
```

---

### 3.10 Verify the Installer Version

Run:

```bash
openshift-install version
```

The output should identify the OKD release for which the installer is configured.

For example, it should contain information corresponding to:

```text
4.22.0-okd-scos.7
```

The installer is release-specific: the installer binary is preconfigured for a particular OKD release. The OKD release page exposes this information along with the release digest. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

---

### 3.11 Verify Both Tools

Run:

```bash
which oc
which kubectl
which openshift-install
```

Expected:

```text
/usr/local/bin/oc
/usr/local/bin/kubectl
/usr/local/bin/openshift-install
```

Then:

```bash
oc version --client
openshift-install version
```

At this point, both primary OKD installation tools should be available from any directory.

---

### 3.12 Verify the Downloaded Archives

List the downloaded archives:

```bash
ls -lh *.tar.gz
```

You should have:

```text
openshift-client-linux-4.22.0-okd-scos.7.tar.gz
openshift-install-linux-4.22.0-okd-scos.7.tar.gz
```

The archives can now be retained for reference or removed after successful installation.

---

### 3.13 Checksum Verification

The OKD release publishes SHA-256 checksums for its release assets. The release page specifically recommends verifying downloaded archives using checksum/signature information. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

Calculate the checksum of the downloaded client archive:

```bash
sha256sum openshift-client-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

Calculate the checksum of the installer archive:

```bash
sha256sum openshift-install-linux-${OKD_RELEASE}.tar.gz
```

Compare the resulting SHA-256 values with the values published for the exact release asset.

> The checksum must match the checksum published for the same release and exact filename. Do not compare against a checksum from a different OKD release.

---

### 3.14 Final Tool Verification

Run the complete verification:

```bash
echo "Architecture:"
uname -m

echo
echo "OKD Release:"
echo "$OKD_RELEASE"

echo
echo "oc:"
which oc
oc version --client

echo
echo "openshift-install:"
which openshift-install
openshift-install version
```

The expected state is:

```text
Architecture:
x86_64

OKD Release:
4.22.0-okd-scos.7

oc:
/usr/local/bin/oc
<matching client version>

openshift-install:
/usr/local/bin/openshift-install
<matching installer version>
```

### 3.15 Installation Tool Checklist

Before continuing:

```text
[ ] Exact OKD release selected
[ ] Installation host architecture verified
[ ] OKD client downloaded
[ ] OKD client extracted
[ ] oc installed
[ ] oc version verified
[ ] OKD installer downloaded
[ ] OKD installer extracted
[ ] openshift-install installed
[ ] openshift-install version verified
[ ] SHA-256 checksums verified
[ ] Both commands are available through PATH
```

The installation environment is now ready for the next stage.

